 ### [Statistical Metrics-based Transfer Function](9488-statistical-metrics-based-transfer-function.md)
-This method classifies volume data by mapping voxel intensities to optical properties using local statistical metrics (such as mean and variance) computed over a neighborhood, rather than relying solely on the scalar value at the voxel center. It addresses the problem of feature separation in regions where scalar values overlap but local statistical properties differ, offering a semi-automatic mechanism for selecting neighborhoods to extract these properties. It is distinguished from sibling methods like the Gaussian Sum Transfer Function by its explicit use of first- and second-order local statistics (mean, variance) as the primary classification domain, enabling the separation of fine structures based on their local homogeneity or heterogeneity. See [Statistical Metrics-based Transfer Function](9488-statistical-metrics-based-transfer-function.md).
+Statistical Metrics-based Transfer Function is a classification method that maps volume data to optical properties using local statistical features—such as mean and variance—computed from a voxel's neighborhood, rather than relying solely on the voxel's scalar intensity. It solves the problem of separating features with similar scalar values but distinct local structures (e.g., fine textures or edges) by integrating spatial context into the transfer function domain. Unlike standard scalar-based transfer functions, it incorporates neighborhood statistics, and unlike higher-order moment analysis, it typically focuses on first and second-order statistics (mean, variance) as the primary delineation tools.
 
 ### [Distribution Families for Differentiable Rendering](11686-distribution-families-for-differentiable-rendering.md)
-This family provides a collection of smooth, differentiable cumulative distribution functions (CDFs) and probability density functions (PDFs) used as transfer functions in differentiable volume rendering. It solves the problem of non-differentiable discontinuities in traditional hard classification by replacing step functions with continuous, parameterized curves that allow gradients to flow through the opacity and color assignment during backpropagation. What sets this family apart from its siblings is the explicit enumeration of diverse mathematical families (e.g., Cauchy, Gumbel, Gamma) and their variants (reversed, square-root) to provide a unified library of basis functions for learning-based or manual transfer function design, rather than deriving a single statistical model from data. See [Distribution Families for Differentiable Rendering](11686-distribution-families-for-differentiable-rendering.md).
